1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,480
Mars has barely any air.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,880
So how did NASA fly a helicopter there?

3
00:00:04,180 --> 00:00:06,340
Mars's atmosphere is only about

4
00:00:06,340 --> 00:00:09,040
one percent as dense as Earth's.

5
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:12,140
That makes generating lift incredibly difficult.

6
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,680
Meet Ingenuity, a tiny aircraft weighing

7
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:17,950
just 1.8 kilograms.

8
00:00:18,250 --> 00:00:21,260
Ingenuity used two counter-rotating rotors,

9
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:24,800
spinning at roughly 2,500 revolutions per minute,

10
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,910
to push enough air downward.

11
00:00:27,210 --> 00:00:29,490
NASA planned just five test flights.

12
00:00:29,790 --> 00:00:33,140
Ingenuity ended up flying 72 times.

13
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:36,960
A small experiment designed to prove one thing

14
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:39,800
became the first aircraft to achieve powered,

15
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:42,200
controlled flight on another planet.
